::STAT_U02_L01_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L01_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
What is the mean of 4, 6, 8, 10?
{
~A. 6#This is one data value, not the average of all values.
~B. 8#This is one data value, not the mean.
~C. 28#This is the sum before dividing by the number of values.
=D. 7#The sum is 28, and 28 divided by 4 is 7.
}

::STAT_U02_L01_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L01_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
What is the median of 3, 5, 7, 9, 21?
{
~A. 5#This is below the middle value.
~B. 9#This is above the middle value.
=C. 7#The values are already ordered, and 7 is the middle value.
~D. 21#This is the largest value, not the median.
}

::STAT_U02_L01_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L01_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
A data set has an outlier much larger than the other values. Which measure of center is usually pulled upward the most?
{
=A. Mean#The mean uses every value, so a large outlier can pull it upward.
~B. Median#The median is resistant because it depends on position.
~C. Mode#The mode depends on repetition, not the size of the outlier.
~D. Range#Range is a measure of variability, not center.
}

::STAT_U02_L01_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L01_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
For 2, 4, 4, 6, 9, what is the mode?
{
=A. 4#The value 4 occurs most often.
~B. 2#2 occurs only once.
~C. 6#6 occurs only once.
~D. 9#9 occurs only once.
}

::STAT_U02_L01_GP_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L01_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
A typical home price is being reported for a neighborhood with one extremely expensive mansion. Which measure of center is usually better?
{
~A. Mean#The mansion can pull the mean upward and make it less typical.
=B. Median#The median is less affected by an extremely high outlier.
~C. Mode#There may be no repeated home price.
~D. Range#Range describes spread, not a typical price.
}
